  "query_preview": "..\nAnother round of high-end scattered showers for Guam and Rota and\nnumerous showers for Saipan and Tinian is expected this evening, \nslightly tapering down after midnight. The back-end of Tropical \nDepression 24W (discussed in the Tropical Systems section below) is \nthe culprit for widespread cloud cover and towering convection this \nevening. Isolated thunderstorms and locally heavy rainfall are still \npossible tonight as TD 24W continues moving north and away from the \nislands. As for the long-term, scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms remain a possibility for all islands through the entire\nforecast period as the monsoon trough lifts north, trailing behind \nTD 24W. The zones remained split to convey a slight difference in \nshower coverage over Guam and Rota versus Saipan and Tinian should \nthe monsoon trough's northward motion be relatively slow. In \naddition, wind directions appear to be slightly different between the\ntwo zones. There is still high uncertainty regarding flooding \nconcerns as ensembles greatly differ in terms of intensity and \ndistribution of rainfall. This situation will continue to be \nmonitored over the coming week.",
